le_sha256" "e11bd9ab4e23c61eec8ff07fff2c56eeb1ad5401031bb55dcaf9935730894b33"} {"question" "q-7602" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{3}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1}=1"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1}=1"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/state.json" "file_sha256" "e11bd9ab4e23c61eec8ff07fff2c56eeb1ad5401031bb55dcaf9935730894b33"} {"question" "q-7602" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{1}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/state.json" "file_sha256" "e11bd9ab4e23c61eec8ff07fff2c56eeb1ad5401031bb55dcaf9935730894b33"} {"question" "q-7601" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/state.json" "file_sha256" "e11bd9ab4e23c61eec8ff07fff2c56eeb1ad5401031bb55dcaf9935730894b33"} {"question" "q-7601" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=16$ and ${b}^{2}=25.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=16,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=25,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{25}=5.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $b=5,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $a=4.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 5=10,$ and the minor axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 4=8.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/state.json" "file_sha256" "e11bd9ab4e23c61eec8ff07fff2c56eeb1ad5401031bb55dcaf9935730894b33"} {"question" "q-7601" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc15c1702908581a3f9250f6b45aa3ccb192cab00b0fc4b0335f321f9ece50ae"} {"question" "q-7601" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/state.json" "file_sha256" "e11bd9ab4e23c61eec8ff07fff2c56eeb1ad5401031bb55dcaf9935730894b33"} {"question" "q-7601" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$10$ and $8$"] ["text" "$25$ and $16$"] ["text" "$5$ and $4$"] ["text" "$50$ and $32$"] ["text" "$\\sqrt{5}$ and $2$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/state.json" "file_sha256" "e11bd9ab4e23c61eec8ff07fff2c56eeb1ad5401031bb55dcaf9935730894b33"} {"question" "q-7601" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$10$ and $8$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/state.json" "file_sha256" "e11bd9ab4e23c61eec8ff07fff2c56eeb1ad5401031bb55dcaf9935730894b33"} {"question" "q-279174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "For the ellipse $\\frac{{x}^{2}}{12}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}=1,$ find the lengths of its major and minor axes.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the major axis is \n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ The length of the minor axis is \n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/state.json" "file_sha256" "e11bd9ab4e23c61eec8ff07fff2c56eeb1ad5401031bb55dcaf9935730894b33"} {"question" "q-279174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=12$ and ${b}^{2}=9.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=12,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{12}=2\\sqrt{3}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=9,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{9}=3.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=2\\sqrt{3},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=3.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3}=4\\sqrt{3},$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 3=6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/state.json" "file_sha256" "e11bd9ab4e23c61eec8ff07fff2c56eeb1ad5401031bb55dcaf9935730894b33"} {"question" "q-279174" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc15c1702908581a3f9250f6b45aa3ccb192cab00b0fc4b0335f321f9ece50ae"} {"question" "q-279174" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/state.json" "file_sha256" "e