$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{36}=6.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=5,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nThus, the semi-major axis has length $a=6,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\sqrt{5}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 6=12,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{5}=2\\sqrt{5}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/state.json" "file_sha256" "e11bd9ab4e23c61eec8ff07fff2c56eeb1ad5401031bb55dcaf9935730894b33"} {"question" "q-279172" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc15c1702908581a3f9250f6b45aa3ccb192cab00b0fc4b0335f321f9ece50ae"} {"question" "q-279172" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/state.json" "file_sha256" "e11bd9ab4e23c61eec8ff07fff2c56eeb1ad5401031bb55dcaf9935730894b33"} {"question" "q-279172" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/state.json" "file_sha256" "e11bd9ab4e23c61eec8ff07fff2c56eeb1ad5401031bb55dcaf9935730894b33"} {"question" "q-279172" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/state.json" "file_sha256" "e11bd9ab4e23c61eec8ff07fff2c56eeb1ad5401031bb55dcaf9935730894b33"} {"question" "q-279172" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\sqrt{5}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/state.json" "file_sha256" "e11bd9ab4e23c61eec8ff07fff2c56eeb1ad5401031bb55dcaf9935730894b33"} {"question" "q-7597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation \n$\\frac{{x}^{2}}{20}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/state.json" "file_sha256" "e11bd9ab4e23c61eec8ff07fff2c56eeb1ad5401031bb55dcaf9935730894b33"} {"question" "q-7597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have ${a}^{2}=20$ and ${b}^{2}=16.$\n\n- Since ${a}^{2}=20,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{20}=2\\sqrt{5}.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=16,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{16}=4.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=2\\sqrt{5},$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=4.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2\\sqrt{5}=4\\sqrt{5},$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot 4=8.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/state.json" "file_sha256" "e11bd9ab4e23c61eec8ff07fff2c56eeb1ad5401031bb55dcaf9935730894b33"} {"question" "q-7597" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc15c1702908581a3f9250f6b45a