}=4,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{4}=2.$\n\n- Since ${b}^{2}=2,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=2,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a=2\\cdot 2=4,$ and the minor axis has length $2b=2\\cdot \\sqrt{2}=2\\sqrt{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/state.json" "file_sha256" "e11bd9ab4e23c61eec8ff07fff2c56eeb1ad5401031bb55dcaf9935730894b33"} {"question" "q-16452" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "fc15c1702908581a3f9250f6b45aa3ccb192cab00b0fc4b0335f321f9ece50ae"} {"question" "q-16452" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/state.json" "file_sha256" "e11bd9ab4e23c61eec8ff07fff2c56eeb1ad5401031bb55dcaf9935730894b33"} {"question" "q-16452" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$2\\sqrt{2}$ and $2$"] ["text" "$2\\sqrt{2}$ and $\\sqrt{2}$"] ["text" "$4$ and $2$"] ["text" "$4$ and $2\\sqrt{2}$"] ["text" "$8$ and $4$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/state.json" "file_sha256" "e11bd9ab4e23c61eec8ff07fff2c56eeb1ad5401031bb55dcaf9935730894b33"} {"question" "q-16452" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$4$ and $2\\sqrt{2}$"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/state.json" "file_sha256" "e11bd9ab4e23c61eec8ff07fff2c56eeb1ad5401031bb55dcaf9935730894b33"} {"question" "e-5071" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the equation of the ellipse given below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/assets/6d2ba3fba1a05a841a486553ea3a52d6f3797974630420ad79ae50f4c6f5bc15.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/example-5071.json" "file_sha256" "31f558e5e74561cd6d5891e655df75e5485cff0910f1b9bd23f6278ddf582093"} {"question" "e-5071" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=5$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{25}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/example-5071.json" "file_sha256" "31f558e5e74561cd6d5891e655df75e5485cff0910f1b9bd23f6278ddf582093"} {"question" "e-5072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the equation of the ellipse given below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/assets/3e8d5ed4bf055960b50e046c0688a3c4cc69907ebb1209bb9658ac4bcba48ffe.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/example-5072.json" "file_sha256" "2f33182a2dd7c674cd899916c2cca8cb8b7eee4678cf441479d0434ca0e8f95e"} {"question" "e-5072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=2$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4.$ Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/example-5072.json" "file_sha256" "2f33182a2dd7c674cd899916c2cca8cb8b7eee4678cf441479d0434ca0e8f95e"} {"question" "e-5073" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation $\\dfrac{x^2}{36} + \\dfrac{y^2}{25} = 1?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/example-5073.json" "file_sha256" "2947a685c969713e284c49a0db1f83af18d4f185fe68c035276a3a3b4083c82b"} {"question" "e-5073" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn the given equation, we have $a^2=36$ and $b^2=25.$\n\n- Since $a^2=36,$ the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{36} = 6.$\n\n- Since $b^2=25,$ the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{25} = 5.$\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=6,$ and the semi-minor axis has length $b=5.$\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length $2a = 2 \\cdot 6 = 12,$ and the minor axis has length $2b = 2 \\cdot 5 =10.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14039348/example-5073.json" "file_sha256" "2947a685c969713e284c49a0db1f83af18d4f185fe68c035276a3a3b4083c82b"}] "usage" {"e-5071" [] "q-73619" [] "q-7604" [] "q-16451" [] "e-5072" [] "q-7603" [] "e-5073" []} "basis" 1359 "retractions" [[17592186088268 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/848/Source/Images/q-7604.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?"] [17592186267491 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13957964/assets/8637901efc126236b190e7012cf65a06be078cb21c3093eff39b927493000138.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?"] [17592186088267 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/848/Source/Images/q-7603.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?"] [17592186267445 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13957964/assets/6116ecf00a26d9828b238879b61385c1296f33ff863dbb8337a3b22be5981f10.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the ellipse given above?"] [17592186087450 :question/problem "What is the equation of the ellipse given below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/848/Source/Images/e5e3e1baad54a5ce65047fbcb5e7a58d.png)"] [17592186087450 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1\n$$\n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=5$ and the vertical radius has length $b=3$. Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{a^{2}} + \\frac{y^{2}}{b^{2}} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{5^{2}} + \\frac{y^{2}}{3^{2}} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{25} + \\frac{y^{2}}{9} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186087451 :question/problem "What is the equation of the ellipse given below?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/848/Source/Images/10eddd924b3dc68fbf1bb4eaeb8f92c5.png)"] [17592186087451 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1\n$$\n\nHere, the horizontal radius has length $a=2$ and the vertical radius has length $b=4$. Therefore, the equation of the ellipse is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{x^{2}}{a^{2}} + \\frac{y^{2}}{b^{2}} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{2^{2}} + \\frac{y^{2}}{4^{2}} &= 1 \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{4} + \\frac{y^{2}}{16} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186087452 :question/problem "What are the lengths of the major and minor axes of an ellipse with equation $\\dfrac{x^2}{36} + \\dfrac{y^2}{25} = 1$?"] [17592186087452 :question/worked-solution "Remember that the equation of an ellipse centered at the origin with a horizontal radius of length $a$ and a vertical radius of length $b$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1\n$$\n\nIn the given equation, we have $a^2=36$ and $b^2=25$.\n\n- Since $a^2=36$, the length of the horizontal radius must be $a=\\sqrt{36} = 6$.\n- Since $b^2=25$, the length of the vertical radius must be $b=\\sqrt{25} = 5$.\n\nSo the semi-major axis has length $a=6$, and the semi-minor axis has length $b=5$.\n\nTherefore, the major axis has length\n\n$$\n2a = 2 \\cdot 6 = 12\n$$\n\nand the minor axis has length\n\n$$\n2b = 2 \\cdot 5 =10\n$$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186086534 17592186086535 17592186086536 17592186086537 17592186086538 17592186086540 17592186086541 17592186086542 17592186086543 17592186086544 17592186267447 17592186267448 17592186267449 17592186267450 17592186267451 17592186267493 17592186267494 17592186267495 17592186267496 17592186267497] "immutable_fields" [[17592186086533 :answer-field/correct] [17592186086533 :answer-field/id] [175